  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crimes scan` reports **`crime_00018` — `hardcoded_localhost` (\"Dev-Server URL\")** at `src/api/files.ts:8`, severity `medium` (0.6), confidence `0.9`, effort `quick`.\n\nThe evidence quotes the literal as:\n\n> ``` `localhost:3000` @L8 ```\n\nIn source that's the full constant backing the new helper:\n\n```ts\n// src/api/files.ts:8\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";\n\nexport function adminUrl(): string {\n  return ADMIN_DASHBOARD;\n}\n```\n\nThe detector's own summary is the right framing: *\"These literals only work against whichever dev server happens to be running on that port — every other environment silently fails to connect.\"*\n\n## Block or request changes?\n\n**Request changes — and don't merge until it's fixed.** Not because the finding is severe in isolation (it's medium/quick), but because of what this particular helper does with the value.\n\n`adminUrl()` is exported and returns a *user-facing destination*, not a fetch target. A wrong fetch URL fails loudly — connection refused, someone gets paged. A wrong dashboard link fails **silently and per-user**: it renders into an email, a redirect, or an admin nav item, and every recipient's browser dutifully tries `http://localhost:3000/admin` on *their own machine*. Most see a connection error and file a confusing bug; anyone running an unrelated local dev server on :3000 sees someone else's app. That's a bad-failure-mode bug, not a hygiene nit.\n\nTwo things make this cheap to fix right now:\n\n- `blast_radius: 0` / `blast_radius_direct_importers: 0` — nothing imports `adminUrl()` yet. This is the last moment the fix is a one-line change rather than a call-site sweep.\n- `effort: quick`. There's no schedule argument for deferring it.\n\nI would not escalate past \"request changes\" to a hard reject: the design of the helper is fine, only the source of its value is wrong.\n\n## Where the URL should actually go\n\n\"Use config\" isn't actionable, so concretely — this repo is plain Node + TypeScript (`node:fs` imports, a `bin` in `package.json`) with `.tsx` route files and no framework config module today. So the mechanism is:\n\n**1. Env var name: `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL`**\n\nNot `API_URL` (which the detector's generic remediation text suggests). This isn't the API base — it's one specific destination, and collapsing it into a shared `API_URL` means the next person deriving `${API_URL}/admin` re-couples the admin console's path to the API host. Name the thing it is.\n\n**2. Settings module: create `src/config/settings.ts`**\n\nThere is no config module in this repo yet, which is why the literal ended up inline. Add one and make it fail fast at import time:\n\n```ts\n// src/config/settings.ts\nfunction required(name: string): string {\n  const value = process.env[name];\n  if (!value) throw new Error(`Missing required env var: ${name}`);\n  return value;\n}\n\nexport const settings = {\n  adminDashboardUrl: required(\"ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL\"),\n} as const;\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/api/files.ts\nimport { settings } from \"../config/settings\";\n\nexport function adminUrl(): string {\n  return settings.adminDashboardUrl;\n}\n```\n\nModule-level evaluation is the point: a missing var crashes the process on boot in staging, rather than quietly emitting a localhost link at 3am.\n\n**3. `.env.example` carries the dev default, the code does not**\n\n```\n# .env.example\nADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:3000/admin\n```\n\nSpecifically **reject** the tempting inline variant:\n\n```ts\nprocess.env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL ?? \"http://localhost:3000/admin\"  // don't\n```\n\nThat's the same bug with extra steps — production silently falls through to the dev value instead of failing. The dev-only default belongs in `.env.example` / `.env.local`, where forgetting to set it in a real environment is loud.\n\n**4. If this file ever gets bundled for the browser, the runtime-config slot matters**\n\n`src/routes/settings/billing.tsx` suggests client-rendered routes exist. Server-only `process.env` reads are stripped in a client bundle, so pick the framework's slot deliberately:\n\n| Runtime | Slot |\n|---|---|\n| Plain Node (current state) | `process.env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` inside `src/config/settings.ts` |\n| Next.js App Router | `NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL`, read in a validated `src/env.ts` (avoid the legacy `publicRuntimeConfig`) |\n| Vite | `VITE_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` via `import.meta.env` |\n\nSince `adminUrl()` currently lives in `src/api/`, keep it server-side and pass the value down — that avoids leaking a public env var at all.\n\n## One adjacent item for the same review\n\n`crime_00019` (`hardcoded_local_path`) sits on the very next line — `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` at `src/api/files.ts:9`, same severity and confidence. It may predate this PR, so it's your call whether to scope it in, but it's the identical failure class (a literal that works on exactly one machine) and it lands in the same config module you're about to create: `SCHEMA_PATH` → `settings.planSchemaPath`, sourced from `PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` with a repo-relative default. Fixing both in one pass is strictly cheaper than two round trips.",
